From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:11:13 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] hk: merge loads of one uniform buffer record

A shader that reads members of one record of a uniform buffer array as
separate expressions, as Counter-Strike 2's depth-of-field gather does
with taps[i].xy, taps[i].z and taps[i].w in a 48-tap loop, got a load
for each: once lowered to global loads, their 64-bit addresses
(base + u2u64(48 + 16 * i), + u2u64(56 + 16 * i), ...) no longer show
that they are the same record, so the vectorizer could not merge them.

Before lowering, make component reads of a uniform buffer vector loads
of the whole vector and mark the loads reorderable, as load_ubo always
is: a uniform buffer does not change during a draw or dispatch. CSE then
merges the repeated loads, and the vectorizer, run on the deref offsets,
merges neighbouring ones.

tests/vulkan_ubo_record_loop.c renders that loop twice, once as the game
writes it and once with each record read once, checks that the images
are identical and prints the cost ratio: on the M4 Pro 1.31 before this
change (69 against 64 instructions), 1.18 after (65 against 64). The
rest is the loop-invariant scale read, which stays in the loop: the game
does not enable robustBufferAccess, so it may not be read speculatively.
In a benchmark of the game's shader at 2560x1440 with 48 taps a pass
goes from 5.35 to 4.85 ms. A sample of dEQP-VK.ubo, robustness2 uniform
and binding_model uniform buffer cases (4002) passes as before.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
